    topic: "Why companies buy",
    level: 1,
    type: "qa",
    classic: true,
    q: "Why would a company acquire another company?",
    a: "To create more value than it could alone. Cost synergies come from combining overlapping operations and revenue synergies from selling more together. A deal can also buy growth, market share, a new market or technology and talent faster than building them. It works only if those benefits are worth more than the premium paid.",
    why: "Every acquisition is a build-or-buy decision. Building takes time and may fail; buying is fast and more certain but costs a control premium over the target's standalone value. So the test isn't whether the target is a good business but whether this buyer can make it worth more than it pays, through synergies, better management or a stronger position. Weaker reasons include diversification, which shareholders can do more cheaply themselves, empire building and chasing EPS accretion for its own sake.",
    trap: "Don't offer diversification as a reason without the caveat: investors can diversify on their own, which is one reason conglomerates often trade at a discount. The follow-up: what's the most a buyer should pay?"
