    q: "What's the difference between a merger and an acquisition?",
    a: "Legally, in a merger two companies combine and only one survives; in an acquisition, one company buys another's shares or assets and often keeps it as a subsidiary. In practice the words are used loosely: 'merger' suggests similar-sized companies combining in stock, 'acquisition' a larger buyer taking control, often for cash.",
