    topic: "Tax-free reorganizations",
    level: 3,
    type: "qa",
    q: "When can a target's shareholders receive acquirer stock without paying tax, and what does the buyer give up in return?",
    a: "When the deal qualifies as a tax-free reorganization under US rules, which mainly requires paying largely in acquirer stock. Holders then defer tax on the stock until they sell it; any cash they receive, called boot, is taxable. In return the buyer gets no step-up: it keeps the target's old tax basis in its assets.",
    why: "Tax law treats a stock-for-stock deal as the same investment continuing in a new form rather than a sale, provided enough of the price is stock (a common rule of thumb is at least about 40%), the target's business continues and the deal has a business purpose. Because no one is taxed on the gain, the asset basis doesn't reset either. Only a taxable purchase of assets, actual or deemed, steps it up; a taxable purchase of shares doesn't. So a stock deal can suit the seller's taxes and cost the buyer future deductions.",
    trap: "Don't assume every stock deal is tax-free. If the stock portion is too small, the deal fails the tests and even the stock received is taxable. That's one reason boards weigh the form of payment, not just the headline price."
