    level: 3,
    type: "qa",
    q: "What happens if an acquirer pays less than the fair value of the target's identifiable net assets?",
    a: "It's a bargain purchase, sometimes called negative goodwill. The acquirer first rechecks that it has identified and measured every asset and liability correctly. Whatever shortfall survives is recorded as a gain in net income at closing, not as negative goodwill on the balance sheet. It's rare and usually comes from a distressed or forced sale.",
    why: "The standards assume buyers don't normally pay less than fair value, so an apparent bargain first suggests an error: missed liabilities, overvalued assets or unrecorded claims. Only what survives that review becomes a gain, under both US GAAP and IFRS. Bankers strip it out of adjusted EPS as one-time and non-cash. Genuine bargains happen when a seller must sell fast, as in a bankruptcy, a regulatory order or a parent in distress, or when fair values include assets the buyer can't easily turn into cash.",
    example: "A buyer pays [[$200M]] for a distressed target whose identifiable net assets are worth [[$240M]] at fair value, after write-ups and deferred taxes. It records no goodwill and a $40M gain at closing.",
    trap: "A bargain purchase gain isn't proof the buyer created $40M of value; it often reflects risks that fair values miss. And older rules that reduced asset values or amortized negative goodwill no longer apply."
