    why: "The same price can produce very different reported earnings. Put more into customer relationships with a short life and amortization rises; put more into goodwill and GAAP EPS looks better but impairment risk grows. That's why independent valuation firms usually prepare the allocation and auditors test it, and why many buyers report adjusted or cash EPS excluding acquired-intangible amortization. In a US asset deal, goodwill and most acquired intangibles are deducted over 15 years for tax, while equipment often depreciates faster, so the split also shifts the timing of tax savings.",
    example: "[[$300M]] allocated to intangibles with a [[10]]-year life adds $30M of amortization a year, $22.5M after a [[25%]] tax rate: $0.09 of EPS on [[250M]] shares. Allocated to goodwill, it costs nothing unless impaired.",
