    topic: "Stock vs asset deals",
    level: 1,
    type: "qa",
    classic: true,
    q: "What's the difference between a stock deal and an asset deal, and which does each side prefer?",
    a: "In a stock deal the buyer buys the target's shares, inheriting every liability, and the assets' tax basis carries over. In an asset deal it buys chosen assets and liabilities, and the tax basis steps up to the price paid. Buyers prefer asset deals for the tax deductions and the ability to leave liabilities behind; sellers usually prefer stock deals.",
    why: "For a US C corporation, an asset sale is taxed twice: the company pays tax on the gain over its asset basis, then its shareholders pay again when the proceeds are distributed. A stock sale is taxed once, at the shareholder level. The buyer's step-up creates extra depreciation and amortization deductions worth real cash, so it will pay more for an asset deal; the question is whether that benefit exceeds the seller's extra tax. Asset deals are also harder to execute, since contracts, permits and licenses may need consent to transfer.",
    trap: "Public-company acquisitions are almost always stock deals, usually done as mergers. Asset deals are common for divisions and for pass-through entities such as S corporations and LLCs, which have no corporate-level tax to double up."
