    q: "A buyer pays $600M for a target whose assets have a $200M tax basis. At a 25% tax rate and a 10% discount rate, what is an asset deal's step-up worth if it's amortized over 15 years?",
    a: "About $51M. The $400M step-up gives $26.7M of extra deductions a year for 15 years, saving $6.7M of tax a year, $100M in total. Discounted at 10%, those savings are worth about $51M today, so the buyer could pay up to that much more for an asset deal than for a stock deal.",
    why: "A step-up turns part of the price into future tax deductions. In the US, goodwill and most acquired intangibles are amortized for tax over 15 years (Section 197), and equipment follows depreciation schedules that are often faster. So the saving arrives slowly and is worth well under its headline. The deductions matter only if the buyer has taxable income to use them. The seller's side matters too: if an asset sale costs the seller more tax, the two sides negotiate over who keeps the net benefit.",
    formula: "Step-up = purchase price − tax basis of the assets\nAnnual tax saving = step-up ÷ amortization years × tax rate\nValue = PV of the annual tax savings",
    example: "[[$600M]] − [[$200M]] = $400M. $400M ÷ [[15]] = $26.7M of deductions a year; × [[25%]] = $6.67M of tax saved a year. The 15-year annuity factor at [[10%]] is 7.61, so the value is $6.67M × 7.61 = $50.7M.",
    trap: "Don't value it at $400M × 25% = $100M: that ignores timing, which roughly halves it here. And in a stock deal there's no step-up unless the parties make a 338(h)(10) or similar election."
