    q: "An acquirer with 240M shares issues 60M new shares to buy a target. What does each side own afterward, and why does it matter?",
    a: "The acquirer's holders own 80% (240M ÷ 300M) and the target's holders 20% (60M ÷ 300M). The split decides how the combined company's future earnings, synergies and risks are shared, who controls it and whether a vote is needed: US exchanges generally require the acquirer's holders to approve issuing 20% or more of its existing shares.",
    why: "In a stock deal the ownership split is the real price: the target's holders receive a fifth of everything the combined company will ever earn, synergies included. Compare it with each side's contribution of earnings and value to see who is paying whom. If the target's holders end up with more than half, they control the combined company, and accounting may treat the legal target as the acquirer, a reverse acquisition. In mergers of equals, the split also shapes board seats.",
    example: "[[240M]] + [[60M]] = 300M shares. The 60M new shares are 25% of the acquirer's existing 240M, above the 20% threshold, so its shareholders must approve the issuance, even though they keep 80%.",
    trap: "Measure the 20% test against shares outstanding before the deal, not after: here 60M is 20% of the combined count but 25% of the existing one. Use diluted shares on both sides when computing ownership."
