    why: "Contribution analysis compares what each side brings with what each side gets. It's used mainly in stock mergers, especially mergers of equals, to support the exchange ratio in fairness opinions. It ignores growth, synergies and control, so it's a sanity check rather than a valuation. Metrics above net income need care: revenue and EBITDA belong to all capital providers, so if one side carries more debt, its EBITDA share overstates its share of the equity.",
