    q: "What is an earnout, and why would a buyer and seller agree to one?",
    a: "An earnout is part of the price paid later, only if the business hits agreed targets such as revenue or EBITDA over the next one to three years. It bridges a valuation gap: the seller believes its forecast and the buyer doesn't, so the buyer pays for today's performance up front and for the forecast only if it's delivered.",
    why: "Earnouts shift forecast risk back to the seller, who knows the business best, and keep sellers who stay on as managers motivated. They're most common in private deals for fast-growing or hard-to-value businesses. The catch is control: after closing the buyer runs the business, and its choices on spending, pricing and integration affect whether targets are hit, so earnouts often end in disputes. Public deals sometimes use contingent value rights instead, paying out if a milestone such as a regulatory approval is reached. The accounting is its own topic: fair value at closing, then remeasured through earnings.",
    example: "The seller wants [[$800M]], betting EBITDA grows from [[$65M]] to [[$80M]]; the buyer will pay [[$650M]], 10x today's EBITDA. Deal: $650M at closing plus up to $150M if EBITDA reaches $80M within [[2]] years. Each side gets the price it believes in.",
    trap: "Define the metric precisely: accounting policies, integration costs, allocated overhead and what the buyer may or may not change. Vague definitions are the classic source of earnout litigation."
