    a: "In a one-step merger, the target's board approves the deal and holders vote at a meeting, after a proxy statement. In a tender offer, the buyer offers to buy shares directly from holders. It can close faster, often within two months, with no meeting; a second-step merger then buys out anyone who didn't tender.",
    why: "Speed shortens the time a deal is exposed to market swings, rival bids and financing risk. US rules keep a tender offer open at least 20 business days. The buyer sets a minimum condition, usually a majority of shares, and Delaware law then lets it complete the merger without a vote. A one-step merger takes longer because the proxy must be filed, possibly reviewed by the SEC and mailed before the meeting. A tender offer can also go straight to holders when the board refuses, which is why hostile bidders use it.",
    trap: "Holdouts aren't stranded as minority holders: the second-step merger pays them the same price, though in a cash deal they can ask a court to appraise their shares instead. Deals needing long regulatory reviews gain little from a tender offer's speed."
