    q: "How does a poison pill work, and why does it stop a hostile bidder?",
    a: "The board issues rights to every shareholder that trigger if anyone buys more than a set stake, often 10–20%, without board approval. Once triggered, every holder except the bidder can buy new shares at half price, diluting the bidder's stake and value. So bidders don't cross the threshold: they negotiate with the board or try to replace it.",
    why: "The pill is a deterrent, not a weapon: it's almost never triggered. Because the board can redeem the rights for a nominal sum, it turns a hostile bid into a negotiation with the board, and bidders answer with proxy fights to elect directors who will redeem it. Boards can adopt a pill in days without a shareholder vote, so many keep one ready rather than in place. Courts accept pills that buy time and leverage, not ones that block holders from ever considering an offer.",
